WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Launching a child process is not complete when the create call returns.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:12.520
The parent still has to manage startup failure, process state, I/O, exit status, timeouts, cancellation, and cleanup.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.220
Lifecycle management is part of the security and reliability contract.

4
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.980
Startup failure should be reported clearly.

5
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:28.520
The parent should distinguish between failure to start the process, failure inside the child, and a child that exits with a nonzero status according to its own contract.

6
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.620
Those cases may require different diagnostics and cleanup behavior.

7
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.860
Exit status should be interpreted deliberately.

8
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.880
Some tools use zero for success and nonzero for failure.

9
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.980
Others encode categories.

10
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.680
The parent should not treat any child completion as success unless the contract says so.

11
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.060
Output should also be validated before it drives later decisions.

12
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.820
The parent must avoid unreaped children where the platform requires explicit waiting.

13
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.000
It should also avoid waiting forever.

14
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:07.380
Timeouts and cancellation policies protect workflows when a child hangs, stops making progress, or waits for input that will never arrive.

15
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.700
Cleanup should cover pipes, temporary files, partial output, and intermediate state.

16
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.880
If setup fails halfway through, the code should close what it opened and remove what it owns.

17
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:24.660
If a timeout occurs, the parent should decide how the child is stopped and how resources are recovered.

18
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.740
Predictable error reporting matters for operations.

19
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:40.320
Logs should identify the program, phase, safe error category, exit status where appropriate, and cleanup result without exposing sensitive arguments or environment values unnecessarily.
